 on the hook to support our EU  customers they came up with a few  options they said we could consider  expanding with AWS we could consider  spinning up our own data center or we  could consider a new cloud provider so  in the end we went with Google Cloud so  the rest of this tale is to show you why  that was not scary with our foundation  of tooling how we tested our biggest  fears fast and how we uncovered 11 years  of our unknown dependencies lurking in  our infrastructure to successfully ship  a new EU data center in q1 so hub spots  best kept secret our is our amazing  platform infrastructure teams these 14  teams have the mission to make our 60  other product teams as effective and  efficient as possible basically to give  them superpowers  to improve the reliability and  efficiency of our platform and to keep  the free in freemium the foundation of  all of this is that everything at  HubSpot is code have you all ever heard  the expression of pets versus cattle  referring to your servers so pets are  the ones that you baby you take care of  them because they are mission-critical  and they are irreplaceable so they can  destroy your business if they die cattle  are all identical you can shoot one in  the head and another one will take its  place at HubSpot we only have cattle no  pets so our platform supports tooling  for our marketing sales and services  customers this runs on top of a giant  automation and analytics platform that  is processing a ton of data data based  on analytics ads contacts email chat all  sorts of things processing data from all  sorts of sources on behalf  of our customers so how do we manage all  of that Big Data there are many  different types of workloads that are  neat workflows that are needed to make  it all work seamlessly together we have  focused largely on open source because  years ago the cloud was not so mature  and we needed the control to be able to  dig in and tune our data services as we  needed we wanted to be able to touch the  code it was also because of the need to  be able to control costs to keep  freemium free we actually have hundreds  of thousands of free customers so we run  our infrastructure with costs in mind  and over the years we wrote a lot of  automation software to keep our data  infrastructure running smoothly since  scale can mean many things to different  companies here I want to give you some  quick numbers to get a sense of what it  meant when we said we were going multi  cloud and multi-region so HBase is the  heart of HubSpot data our HBase clusters  are processing up to 90 million requests  per second that's nearly high and  processing nearly half a petabyte a day  Kafka is hub spots bloodstream with  Kafka we are handling around a million  requests per second elasticsearch is our  nerve system supporting nearly half a  billion documents the bane of my  existence is MySQL we actually have more  than 500 MySQL clusters and at HubSpot  they grow like mushrooms I think our  engineers can't tell the difference  between a database and a table so that  totals more than 1300 individual VMs and  we have historically had one person  managing all of them he knew them all  personally essentially he had 500 pets  and there were far too many manual  operations to tend to them all so early  last year we decided to change all of  that  which was lucky considering the decision  that we were the move we were going to  need the EU data center later in the  year we needed to orchestrate the life  cycles of those 500 clusters and at the  same time a lot of our relational style  data was too big I mean  to move a lot of that data into HBase to  be able to support the request load we  chose by tests and kubernetes to solve  this problem for us by test is an open  source clustering system to automate  horizontal scaling of MySQL and it was  created by the YouTube team we've become  very active contributors to this project  it essentially is a proxy to sequel  queries  it makes sharding very simple to support  horizontal scaling in an easy way  it also replaced our manual and ansible  plays that we use to handle things like  master/slave fail overs when machines  would fail we run by tests inside of  kubernetes we have years of experience  with maysa for orchestrating dr  containers and JVMs for running our own  applications and we were excited to find  a system to orchestrate our data stores  as easily as our applications and we  could share server resources for those  1,300 VMs instead of relying on  dedicated machines  so our kubernetes migration was already  underway when our CEO came to me  so why did we consider Google we've been  running enough  we've been running HubSpot in AWS for 10  years when Brian came to us and said we  needed to do better by our yo customers  and we needed better integrations with  Adwords and G suite we said it was time  to survey the cloud landscape so  BigTable is the basis for HBase we've  admired BigTable performance numbers for  a long time we put a lot of resources  into HBase and dreamed of a future where  Google would take care of all of those  headaches for us plus if you've ever had  a visit from the Google team or talk to  the BigTable engineers they will walk  into your conference room draw a picture  that looks exactly like this on your  wall board whiteboard drop the  microphone and just leave the room so we  were interested in knowing more we were  also interested in Google because we  were big fans of their open source we  have our own internal commitment to open  source and have published some tools  like blazar in singularity I'll tell you  about a little later  and we were already actively  contributing to by testing kubernetes  our infrastructure team was also  interested in seeing what cool things  they could do using Google's Network  offerings plus the timing was perfect  Google was coming out with a data center  in Frankfurt in q4 so the big reason we  are here I am here is to tell this tale  of the foundation of our tools and how  that helped us to move very quickly I  want to point out to you a few areas  that we needed to update to be able to  support a new cloud provider and a new  region and how this could work for you  too  so first I'll give you some details  about the components that make up our  platform so you understand what I'm  talking about so right now we are  running more than 3000 VMs in the cloud  all of our infrastructure tools  interface with our cloud providers  through a tool called Rainmaker  Rainmaker is the foundation of our  infrastructure as code approach it wraps  all of the cloud primitives like the API  is to spin up spin up and down VMs  attached disks manage storage buckets do  security group management things like  that we use puppet to keep the  configuration for all of our VMs  consistent puppet updates are managed  through github and they are deployed  like any other app in our deployment  pipeline  so the hub swap product is actually made  up of more than 6,000 micro services  jobs and tasks all running on top of  singularity in May sews singularity is  our open source app orchestration  framework that is powered by Apache  massifs we introduced singularity into  our platform a few years ago to remove  the complexity of running and deploying  workloads directly to machines it used  to be that engineers had to worry about  everything everything for their services  and tasks they would worry about  horizontally scaling those services they  would worry about whether the underlying  hardware would fit was failing so  singularity let engineers focus on what  they do best which is writing software  instead of managing infrastructure on  top of our app orchestration framework  we have a highly efficient CI CD  pipeline to make it extremely  for our engineers to build and deploy  code fast  we have over 2,000 active github repos  for all of those micro services and  tasks we have actually archived over  6,000 other repos over the years which  is because we are constantly  experimenting improvement in improving  how HubSpot runs so we wrote blaze our  our build orchestration tool we use it  to run all of we used to run all of our  builds on Jenkins but because of the  explosive growth of our repos and  branches it became too complex to manage  the UI basically turned into a hell  scape for our engineers we addressed  those pains by building blaze are around  github web hooks it automatically  detects new repos and requires no extra  configuration to know exactly what to  build blaze our runs inside of  singularity itself so last year Blizzard  did more than two and a half million  builds and this year we are on track to  run more than 4 million builds we also  wrote our own deploy tool called Orion  Orion makes it simple for our engineers  to deploy workloads into singularity  more than 10,000 times a week last year  we in fact deployed over 900,000 times  to both QA and prod and we put a heavy  investment into the user experience for  Orion because this tool is so critical  for our engineers to get their work done  it sports health checks acceptance and  acceptance tests for example we also  acknowledge that engineers do not write  perfect code so we Pro prioritize mean  time to repair making it simple to  rollback changes as well so now back to  our story on how we expanded to the EU  with Google so to start a project like  this is an important question in any  engineering leaders toolbox is how can  we keep it simple  since HubSpot is made up of thousands of  micro services and jobs making a fully  chartered version of HubSpot in such a  short amount of time would be reckless  and we were not reckless we were  confident but not reckless so we started  by narrowing the focus we  ourselves what was the most important  thing for reliability and performance  for our customers in the EU we already  have approximately 70,000 websites  fronted by CloudFlare across the globe  for fast performance but anytime data is  submitted by our customers customers it  goes back to our origin in Virginia and  if there are issues with the network or  any other issues back at the origin that  data could be lost so it's important  that we did not miss any of the  collecting any usage data so we focused  on analytics click tracking CTAs and  those sorts of collected collection  services so as I said before we have  been AWS customers for 10 years so the  next most important thing to figure out  was what did we fear about Google so we  made a list and there were in fact three  things simply could we deploy to GCP  with the network connection between AWS  and GCP be fast enough and there was  this thing the Google team told us about  called live migrations so to start the  question was could we simply deploy  services to GCP we wanted to make sure  our engineers didn't need to care about  the details of running on Google and  also about running in another region I  mentioned Rainmaker earlier as our VM  management tool  back in 2012 VM management was really  hard to do so we had created Rainmaker  to abstract away AWS console and  primitives and create one simple way to  spin up servers correctly so the thing  that we created six years ago to work  better with AWS became a key factor and  making it easy to work with Google too  so what we did was we looked at the API  calls we needed to make Google so we  could start stopped machine set up disk  configuring networking and this was just  a matter of refactoring our code to  create a second adapter the first  adapter was to handle the AWS primitives  and then a second adapter for Google  primitives the rain make it rain maker  API is all stayed exactly the same so  all of our automated tooling stayed the  same  then we updated the Rainmaker a you I  added Belgium as an option because the  Frankfurt data center didn't exist at  that time and bam we had Google our  second fear was the network connection  between AWS and Google since our data  was now going to be crossing the  Atlantic so we used the standard cloud  provider VPNs to connect the two  networks together across the ocean and  it was giving us approximately one  gigabit per second and taking time to  ramp up to that speed to support HBase  replication and our Kafka streams we  wanted to make sure we could get 10  gigabits per second throughput across  the ocean and we wanted to treat our  European data center just like any other  availability zone within Amazon so the  way that our team did that was actually  rather clever they noticed that they  would get a 6 millisecond and 6  milliseconds of latency between our data  center in Virginia and Google's data  center in Virginia so we set up  strongswan which is an open source VPN  tunnel to connect the Virginia data  centers once the traffic was inside  Google across the Atlantic on Google's  fast internal network instead of over  the open Internet and we were able to  get the performance that we needed the  final thing that we feared were live  migrations and we really feared them  live migrations are actually very clever  when Google needs to replace hardware or  update their hypervisor they move your  VM from one piece of hardware to another  behind-the-scene when we first heard  about this we thought it was really cool  but then we were afraid of what it was  going to do to HBase  so with HBase we keep a strict 6  millisecond response time and this is  important because the HubSpot UI touches  many different services and those  services touch many other services so  any delay in any request can cause a  cascading effect resulting in a slow UI  for our users so even though the  difference between a 6 millisecond  response time and a 10 millisecond  response time doesn't seem like much  that makes a difference between a slow  and snappy  you I for our customers so the way live  migrations work is Google takes a  snapshot of your VM and starts an  identical copy on other hardware then  they pause the original VM copy over  anything that changed and then start up  the new VM the Google folks told us that  this could take up to a second depending  on how much RAM was on the original VM  or how heavy the RAM turn was HBase is  very right intensive meaning the pause  time could end up being very long and  that could be a catastrophic to the  response time so to solve this we spun  up an HBase cluster in Google we  replayed production workloads through  that cluster had the Google engineers  force a bunch of live migrations and we  watched our graphs to see the effects we  were shocked to see there were no  effects at all so at that point our  three fears were gone so we committed to  moving forward with Google in the EU so  now that our fears were gone what was  the plan this was four steps update rain  maker spin up the platform components  work out all the application dependency  issues and finally update our DNS to  send traffic to the EU so it took us  five days to update it took us five days  to set up our infrastructure to deploy  to Frankfurt we had to move from Belgium  to Frankfurt this involves setting up  DNS services as well as puppet rainmaker  was also really easy to set up because  we already had the adapter for Google so  it was simply pointing to a new region  next was the bulk of the work where we  considered HubSpot really multi cloud  and multi region our team spun up the  clusters we needed to run our platform  in Google including zookeeper HBase LDAP  vault we had originally hacked a deploy  option to Belgium early on to get a  proof of concept for Orion to deploy to  Google in the EU  so we rewrote it the right way and  deployed analytics as our first test  service we also got the product  engineering teams excited and showed  them the simple way  could configure their services to run in  both regions or just one or the other so  they stood started deploying at that  time to this took us 30 days to get  right product teams started untangling  their application services at that time  they figured out what they needed to run  to support our data collection services  in the EU independently without reaching  back to the US we had quite a few to  work through at that time and plenty of  skeletons in the closet  apart from spinning up clusters and  deploying services there was actually an  even bigger hurdle and this was the  human element of the project we have  more than 60 small autonomous  engineering teams they are in Dublin  Boston as well as remote teams with so  many micro services we need to figure  out the dependencies and uncover all of  those skeletons as fast as possible so  this was the key question how do you get  everyone to find all of the skeletons so  with the way we did this was with a  month and a half of weekly chaos testing  we decided this was the best way to get  everyone on the same page to figure out  what we didn't know about and to move  fast together so we got an engineer from  each product team who owned the  collection service along with the  platform engineers we bought them all  lunch we dialed in the Dublin team and  we broke the network so that first test  they were all very confident that  everything was going to work but there  was a huge list of things that broke our  goal was to cut the network and be able  to restart all of those services and  ensure that they could survive  completely independently so we made a  list and it was a very long list of  everything that we had broken and all of  the misc dependencies for example  databases that were still running in the  u.s. that needed to be replicated  authentication services domain services  as well so we added those teams to  future tests and the list of what we  needed to fix was long but the teams all  agreed to fix them and come back the  next week so  did this each week for six weeks and  each week the list got shorter and  shorter until there was no more list so  we even came up with some creative ways  to cause failures between the two data  centers and tried those as well until  finally it was all green and we were  confident we could run in the EU data  center completely independent from the  US the final step was to enable  CloudFlare geo debase dns for our  production traffic that took a few days  to roll out because we did each service  one at a time to make sure that there  were no disruptions to our customers so  the overall timeline was in fact longer  than the 30 days I promised in the title  of this talk but we were officially  multi-cloud and multi-region with our  platform and one test service but just  not fully production ready in 30 days  but a short seven weeks later we met our  deadline with room to spare so what was  the result today nine percent of our VMs  are running in Google Cloud we saw a 5x  speed-up in response time for our  European customers due to SSL  terminating in Europe and 17 percent of  our collection requests now are  processed on Google cloud the EU is also  spared to service disruptions when there  were Network issues as well one funny  thing that happened during our testing  was a trans-pacific network cut during  our tests so requests from Australia  we're getting routed to Europe instead  of the US so we were getting a similar  unexpected boost for our customers in  Australia at the same time so at the end  of q1 we celebrated and the team has  moved on to their next big mission after  some great lessons learned about going  multi region and multi-cloud at the same  time so let's recap those key takeaways  list and test your fears first and do  this fast  keep it simple do not bite off more than  you can chew with the first go there  will be plenty of unknowns use chaos  testing as your best friend it is a  great forcing function to  uncover those skeletons and shed light  on what is not done treat your  infrastructure as code use abstractions  to be able to experiment and move fast  and finally don't forget to use lunch to  coerce the human side of the project  thank you  [Music]

